 that spoke to the present  conflict and which honored his personal  and private communion with nature  he realized did not have to be an  either/or proposition he came to this  realization when he liked Burchfield  found himself staring into his past  facing the two hills that had haunted  him since childhood in 1942 while  carrying out his war commissions Bert  Nash visited a friend whose property had  a large window overlooking the Whitnall  clumps as if bringing Nash's own distant  memories back into view the encounter  stimulated a culminating series of  paintings revolving around the site  which were not made in any official  capacity or related to the warm  landscape of the vernal equinox as one  of these paintings draws on all of his  previous imaginative investigations into  the synthesis of oppositional elements  Sun and Moon night and day winter and  spring blue and pink male and female  past and present youth and maturity are  among the possible duality is brought to  bear upon and around these mirrored  hills as if compelled to order the  cosmos itself Nash brings all of these  forces into a new and mysterious  equation the year after he completed the  coming of spring Burchfield returned to  another youthful image autumnal fantasy  subjecting it to to a wild expansion  here he really lets rip - birds sing to  each other their music resounding and it  stretched sonic  Camas from the tree from the tree knots  everything is simultaneous brought to a  crescendo of light and visual sound a  continent away  it was gunshots flying in all directions  and reverberations of falling bombs I'm  not suggesting that Burchfield was  thinking of the war let alone trying to  interpret or redeem it only that he's  given us here in his own cartoon  inspired notation a seriously clamorous  version vision of nature's music I'm not  saying that either of these paintings  are allegories of war but I'm suggesting  it would be equally mistaken to consider  them in their historical context as I'm  doing and conclude that they're escapist  fantasies they are fantasies to be sure  as Burchfield title fully owns but  they're fantasies of the life and death  of nature president every moment as my  friend Michael alig each of birch fields  dry falling leaves as my friend Michael  Rose is written shines with its own  dying  ground air water branch and sunlight  skies shaped with the consciousness of  their place in the mortal fabric war is  unbalanced but as the writer guide  Davenport writes in his brilliant essay  on Burchfield nature is self renewing  and perennial this was the hope at least  that gnashing Burchfield put their faith  in through the darkest days of their own  season so the genius low side we  perceive in their unquiet landscape  seems to be that of the artists own  encompassing spirit  you


